  <h3>JUST FALL BACKWARDS, BRO</h3>

  <p>
    It takes a long time to build and seconds to ruin. And then it takes even longer to rebuild. It’s what we desire from relationships, and it’s an essential ingredient of the glue that holds us together. It’s trust. It’s being able to believe the word of someone else, count on them, and know they’re going to come to your aid, having your back in times of need. However, trust isn’t just a feeling. It’s a nice feeling, but there’s more to it. Trust is built through multiple behaviors, expectations, and choices. It’s not built by good intentions or promises alone; it develops through consistent honesty, transparency, accountability, proof of alliance, and ethical behavior. These five components work together to create reliability and security in relationships, demonstrating that trust must be earned and maintained through actions rather than simply expected from others. It’s confidence developed through their behavior over time.
  </p>

  <p>
    Honesty is a key component of being trustworthy. Telling the truth is paramount for someone to be trusted, because you are worth your word. Transparency is also an important component of trust. But how are they different? They seem interchangeable, but they are different. For example, an honest person will tell the truth, and a transparent person will be forthcoming with relevant information, or an honest person will establish credibility and a transparent person establishes openness. They overlap, but neither automatically guarantees the other. Being honest establishes that someone can believe what you say. Being transparent helps them feel confident that important information isn’t being deliberately hidden.
  </p>

  <p>
    There was a friend of mine who said she would pick me up and take me to a doctor’s appointment after listening to me tell her about how I had no ride. She offered, and I asked her many times if she was sure. She had started drinking again the day before my doctor’s appointment and called someone else to tell them she couldn’t take me anymore, so that person stepped in and was able to drive me. Classic example of the contrast between meaning what you say and doing what you say. She was completely honest about her intentions, but she was completely unreliable. She did apologize later, but I have her pegged as capricious, undependable, and untrustworthy. Her honesty about her intentions gave her credibility, but her accountability demonstrated unreliability. Now imagine someone says, “Just fall backwards.” You’d expect them to have their arms out, ready to catch you. Let’s break this down:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Scenario</th>
        <th>What It Demonstrates</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Your friend promises to catch you and does.</td>
        <td>Accountability and reliability</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Your friend sincerely promises but gets distracted.</td>
        <td>Good intentions without follow-through</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Your friend drops you and blames you for falling wrong.</td>
        <td>Lack of accountability</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    The worst outcome is for someone to blame you for something that isn’t your fault, but it happens all of the time. People also go to the hospital all the time, so I wonder how many cases are from trust falls gone awry.
  </p>

  <p>
    Accountability doesn’t mean never making a mistake. It’s quite the opposite. It’s taking responsibility for your actions when you make a mistake. When someone wrongs you, their actions will say all you need to know about them. Do they admit what happened and apologize? Make excuses? Blame you? Change their behavior? Run and hide from the issue? This is where accountability becomes important to rebuilding trust. Intentions in these situations may also be good, but it’s the outcome and what happens in reality that matters. I can mean to do my dishes and take out my garbage all day, but that doesn’t mean I won’t put the ick in the kitchen with all of the fruit flies and mice that infiltrate. I can lie to a friend with good intentions, but if I’m caught being dishonest, my word no longer means as much or anything and—more importantly—my friend can be hurt, upset, and disappointed. They say, “The road to hell is paved with good intentions.” Something that solidifies trust and maintains it is consistency. Another element is responsibility. Through these two things, it’s possible to demonstrate patterns of being a person who is willing to put in the work for a relationship and that you have someone’s back as well as showing up on time, when you say you will, and for that other person. It sounds easy, but it can be hard in practice for some people.
  </p>

  <p>
    So, the other person has caught you from the trust fall—they literally have your back. This is proof of alliance. Proof of alliance is knowing that someone is in your corner and believing they wouldn’t intentionally hurt, upset, or let you down. One thing, though, is that someone unreliable can still be on your side. Someone can be reliable without necessarily being on your side. Loyalty, though, isn’t people-pleasing—it’s not telling someone what they want to hear or enabling someone. Loyalty also includes looking out for someone, even if they don’t want to hear it. Having someone’s back doesn’t necessarily mean protecting them from the consequences of their own actions or avoiding an uncomfortable conversation. This person supports you when nobody is watching. This is where ethical actions come in.
  </p>

  <p>
    So, are you ready to let go and fall backwards? Pick your person wisely. Do they have:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Honesty</strong>—they aren’t lying about their willingness to catch you?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Transparency</strong>—they tell you if they’re not prepared?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Accountability</strong>—they follow through or own up if they fail?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Proof of alliance</strong>—they genuinely want to protect you?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ethical actions</strong>—they won’t deliberately let you fall for their own amusement?
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    You need all five components to successfully complete the exercise. Trust involves vulnerability, not blind faith. There’s always uncertainty whether you can trust a person, but there’s a difference between accepting that uncertainty and ignoring evidence that someone isn’t trustworthy. If you don’t complete the exercise and fall, how can the relationship, if at all, be repaired? An apology that acknowledges the fall should come, and then changed behavior determines whether or not you’ll fall backwards again. Just remember that forgiveness doesn’t come with a side of concussions and bruises.
  </p>

